---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const tools = await getCollection('tools');
const sorted = tools.sort((a, b) => a.data.name.localeCompare(b.data.name));

// Concepts we cross out — billing practices, not companies.
const deadIdeas = [
  '$49/user/mo',
  'annual lock-in',
  'your data, their cloud',
  'contact sales',
  'feature tiers',
  'price hike #4',
  'export fees',
  'the monthly bill',
];
---

<Base>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="wrap hero-inner">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="hero-victims" aria-label="Subscription-software habits this site argues against">
          {deadIdeas.slice(0, 4).map((idea) => <s>{idea}</s>)}
        </p>
        <h1>
          SaaS is dead.<br />
          <span class="accent">Long live self-hosting.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">
          Most business software is rented: so much per person, per month, forever. What fewer
          people know is that for a lot of it there's a free, open-source version doing the same
          job. The catch used to be the setup. These days you paste a short text into Claude and it
          installs the thing for you. This site lists the good ones, with the texts ready to copy.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-cta">
          <a class="btn" href="#directory">Browse the tools</a>
          <a class="btn-ghost" href="#how">How does this work?</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-stone reveal" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 120 160" role="img">
          <path
            d="M20 150 V60 c0 -25 18 -45 40 -45 s40 20 40 45 v90 z"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="4"></path>
          <text x="60" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-size="26" font-weight="700" fill="currentColor">
            RIP
          </text>
          <text x="60" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="currentColor">SaaS</text>
          <text x="60" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">$99/mo</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="ticker" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="ticker-track">
      {
        [0, 1].map(() => (
          <div class="ticker-group">
            {deadIdeas.map((idea) => (
              <>
                <span class="ticker-item">
                  <s>{idea}</s>
                </span>
                <span class="ticker-sep">☠</span>
              </>
            ))}
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="stats wrap reveal">
    <div class="stat">
      <span class="stat-num">$300<span class="stat-unit">+/mo</span></span>
      <span class="stat-label"
        >what a 10-person team can spend on CRM, video hosting and HR tools combined</span
      >
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <span class="stat-num accent">$0</span>
      <span class="stat-label">what the tools on this site cost. You bring the machine.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <span class="stat-num">~10<span class="stat-unit">min</span></span>
      <span class="stat-label">typical setup time when Claude does the typing</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="how wrap reveal" id="how">
    <p class="section-kicker">how it works</p>
    <h2>Three steps. No coding. Seriously.</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">1</span>
        <h3>Pick a tool</h3>
        <p>
          Find the one that does what you need. There's CRM, video hosting, HR, live streaming —
          the usual suspects.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">2</span>
        <h3>Copy the instructions</h3>
        <p>
          Every tool page has a short text with the full setup recipe. You don't need to read it.
          You don't even need to understand it. Just copy it.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">3</span>
        <h3>Paste it into Claude</h3>
        <p>
          Open <a href="https://claude.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude</a> (or{' '}
          <a href="https://claude.ai/claude-code" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code</a>),
          paste the text, answer a few questions. It installs everything on your machine while you
          watch.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="directory wrap" id="directory">
    <p class="section-kicker reveal">the directory</p>
    <h2 class="reveal">The tools</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      {
        sorted.map((tool) => (
          <a class="card reveal" href={`/tools/${tool.id}`}>
            <div class="card-top">
              <h3>{tool.data.name}</h3>
              <span class="badge">{tool.data.category}</span>
            </div>
            <p class="card-tagline">{tool.data.tagline}</p>
            <p class="card-kills">
              <span class="kills-label">alternative to</span>
              {tool.data.replaces.map((r, i) => (
                <>
                  {i > 0 && ', '}
                  {r.name}
                </>
              ))}
            </p>
            <span class="card-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
              →
            </span>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="price-note reveal">
      Prices mentioned for commercial products are indicative. Vendors change them often — check
      their websites for current numbers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="faq wrap" id="faq">
    <p class="section-kicker reveal">questions, answered</p>
    <h2 class="reveal">"Wait, is this for real?"</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item reveal">
        <summary>Is this legal?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. Every tool here is open source: the people who wrote it published the code under a
          license (MIT, AGPL, GPL — it varies) that explicitly allows anyone to run it, free of
          charge, for personal or business use. Using it this way is exactly what the license
          permits.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two things we're careful about. Product names and logos are trademarks of their owners,
          so we mention commercial products only to compare, never to suggest they endorse us — and
          we don't use their logos. And saasdead is an independent community project, affiliated
          with nobody, open-source or commercial.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item reveal">
        <summary>Do I need to know how to code?</summary>
        <p>
          No. That's the point of the site. Each tool comes with a text you paste into Claude.
          Claude does the typing, you answer its questions. If you can copy and paste, you're
          qualified.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item reveal">
        <summary>What does "hosting on my own machine" mean?</summary>
        <p>
          Instead of running on some company's servers, the software runs on a computer you
          control: your own machine, or a small server you rent for a few dollars a month. Same
          software, different landlord. Your data stays with you.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item reveal">
        <summary>What if something breaks?</summary>
        <p>
          Ask Claude first — paste the error message and it usually sorts it out. If that fails,
          these projects have documentation, forums and issue trackers full of people who hit the
          same wall before you did. They're mature software, used in production by thousands of
          organizations.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item reveal">
        <summary>Why would anyone give this away?</summary>
        <p>
          Depends on the project. Some are built by volunteers who think software should be a
          commons. Some are made by companies that sell hosting or support around the free version.
          Either way, the license is the license: you can use it, forever, at no cost.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
